  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Sensible weather will continue to be quite \nagreeable for outdoor activities. Aside from a ribbon of clouds \nmoving through from north to south this evening, a return to \nsunny days is expected as high pressure slowly builds in through\nthe weekend. Main impacts for this period will be related to \nwinds this evening on Lake Champlain, and then possible fire \nweather concerns over the weekend with critically low relative \nhumidity values likely in parts of the region.\n\nA moisture-starved cold front passed through the region today, \ndetached from a ribbon of higher moisture behind it. Satellite \nimagery shows this cloud cover is tied to a corridor of higher \nrelative humidity in the 925-850 millibar layer, with the air mass \nabove that layer very dry. Hence, north of this region there are \njust some diurnally driven clouds and we can expect skies in our \nregion to trend clear overnight. \n\nA period of relatively fast pressure rises this evening is \nexpected with accelerated northerly flow through the Champlain \nValley as skies clear out again. Roughly between 7 PM and 10 PM \nwe expect the strongest northerly winds in the 20 to 30 knot \nrange on Lake Champlain with similar gusts in the eastern \nChamplain Valley. Northerly, rather than a more common \nnorthwesterly, flow will continue into Friday with a general \ndecay of wind speeds as high pressure builds in. \n\nThe lightening wind will lead to a chilly night and have leaned\ntowards colder guidance. At perennial cool spot SLK/Adirondack \nRegional Airport, a daily minimum temperature is possible (14 \ndegrees). Any passing high clouds should have little affect on \nsurface temperatures during the period. Also, as temperatures \nmoderate aloft, both Saturday and Sunday will feature the return\nof the typical cold season surface inversion with mountain \nsummits relative mild. Generally Sunday morning looks a bit less\ncold than Saturday morning as light south winds will interfere\nwith the nocturnal boundary layer.\n\nForecast soundings through the next three days show very dry air\naloft and correspondingly anomalously low precipitable water.\nWhile localized low humidity is expected tomorrow, cool\nnortherly advection doesn't favor particularly low surface\nhumidity at the surface. Dew points in the 20s are expected \nover the next couple of days, and with milder temperatures on \nSaturday reaching the upper 50s in most locations, this is the\nday when most locations will likely see their lowest relative \nhumidities. Slight improvement on Sunday is expected with \nregards to fire weather concerns as some modest moisture\nadvection occurs. Light winds through the weekend should\nminimize fire spread, however.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The stretch of quiet and dry weather looks \ncontinue into next week as the region remains under the \ninfluence of high pressure. The ridge axis will shift slightly \neastward late Sunday into Monday, allowing for some light \nsouthwest flow and warmer temperatures. Daytime high \ntemperatures on Monday look to warm into the upper 50s to mid \n60s. A weak cold front looks to move through late Monday, but it\nwill be quite moisture starved with no precipitation expected \nfrom this feature. Some cooler air will move in behind the \nfront, with high temperatures on Tuesday expected to be in the \nmid 40s to low 50s. At this time, it looks like the dry stretch \nwill continue through much of next week. Despite the prolonged \nstretch of dryness, winds look to remain light enough to limit \nany fore weather concerns at this time.",
